        await GetOrCreateModule(new TrainingModule
        {
            Title = "Sequences — Gated Steps and Sign-Offs",
            Slug = "sequences-overview",
            Summary = "What a sequence is, how gates hold a step until a condition is met, and how runs are started, cleared, overridden, reworked, and cancelled.",
            ContentType = TrainingContentType.Article,
            EstimatedMinutes = 7,
            IsPublished = true,
            SortOrder = 1,
            AppRoutes = """["/sequences","/sequences/instances"]""",
            Tags = """["sequences","gates","quality","production"]""",
            ContentJson = """{"body":"## Sequences\n\nA **sequence** is an ordered set of steps where some steps cannot begin until something outside the step is true: an inspector has signed off, an approval has gone through, a permit is still valid, a lot has not expired. Sequences give you a place to write that order down once, run it against a job or any other record, and see at a glance which step is waiting on what.\n\nYou find them under **Production > Sequences**. There are two pages: **Definitions** (the templates) and **Instances** (the runs). Both are for Admins and Managers.\n\n### Steps, edges, and gates\n\nA definition is built from three things:\n\n- **Steps** are the work items, in the order you want them to appear. Each has a short key (used to refer to it) and a display name.\n- **Edges** say which step must finish before another can start. A step with no incoming edge is a start step. A step can wait on several earlier steps; its **Join** setting decides whether it needs *all* of them done or *any* one of them.\n- **Gates** are conditions attached to a step. A step is not allowed to start until every gate on it reads **Go**.\n\nA step whose earlier steps are done but which still has a gate that is not Go shows as **Blocked**, with the reason next to it. Blocked is not something anyone sets; it is simply what the run looks like while a gate is holding.\n\n### Where a gate gets its answer\n\nEach gate has a **Source**, which is where it looks for its Go or No-Go:\n\n- **Manual clearance** — someone with the right role presses **Clear** on the gate. The record of that press is the sign-off.\n- **Approval** — Go once the record this run is attached to has an approved approval request.\n- **Time window** — Go only between a start and end date, for things like permit validity.\n- **Resource clock** — Go while a timer on some resource (a lot, a tool, a certificate) has not run out.\n- **Custom** — a condition your Forge has been set up to answer, such as \"materials are on hand.\"\n\nIf a gate cannot get an answer it reads No-Go. Sequences fail closed: an unknown condition never lets a step through.\n\n### Definitions have versions\n\nA definition starts as a **Draft**. You can edit a draft as much as you like. When it is ready you **Publish** it; from then on it cannot be changed. If you need a change, press **New version**, which copies the published definition into a new draft one version higher. Runs already in progress keep using the version they started on. **Retire** stops new runs from starting on a definition; runs already going keep going.\n\nA definition can name a **Subject type** (for example *Job*) and be set to **Auto-start** — every new record of that type gets its own run automatically.\n\n### Running a sequence\n\nOn the **Instances** page, **Start run** picks a published definition and, optionally, the record it is for. The run shows every step with its status: **Pending**, **Ready**, **In progress**, **Complete**, or **Skipped**. When a step is Ready you press **Start**, and when the work is done you press **Complete**. Completing a step is what wakes up the steps after it.\n\nRuns attached to a job also appear in a **Gates** section on the job's detail panel, so the people working the job do not have to leave it.\n\n### Dwell time\n\nA step can carry a **Max dwell** in minutes. Once a step has been in progress that long, the definition's **On dwell expiry** setting says what happens: **Block** holds the run, **Flag** marks it for attention, and **Escalate** notifies the role named in **Escalate role**.\n\n### When things go wrong\n\nFour actions let a supervisor deal with reality, and every one of them asks for a reason that is kept with the run:\n\n- **Override** forces a gate to Go and keeps it there. Use it when the condition is satisfied in a way Forge cannot see.\n- **Skip** marks a step as skipped; the steps after it treat it as done.\n- **Rework** resets a completed step and everything after it back to Pending. Use it when something has to be redone.\n- **Cancel run** ends the run permanently.\n\n**Re-evaluate** is the harmless one: it simply asks Forge to look at every gate again right now, which is useful after you have fixed something outside the sequence.\n\n### The activity list\n\nEvery change to a run — a step starting, a gate clearing, an override, a rework — is written to the run's **Activity** list at the bottom of the panel, with who did it and when. Nothing in a run's history can be edited or removed, so the list is the audit trail.","sections":[]}"""
        });

